                  ,"index": 54
                }
              }
                ,"logicalLocations": [
                    {
                    "fullyQualifiedName": "pkg:maven\/io.netty\/netty-transport@4.2.12.Final"
                    }
                ]
              
            }
          ]
          }, {
          "ruleId": "CVE-2026-47244",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "CVE-2026-47244 - Netty is a network application framework for development of protocol servers and clients. Prior to versions 4.1.135.Final and 4.2.15.Final, DefaultHttp2Connection.DefaultEndpoint initialises maxActiveStreams\/maxStreams to Integer.MAX_VALUE, and Http2Settings never inserts SETTINGS_MAX_CONCURRENT_STREAMS by default (Http2Settings.java:305-307 only clamps a user-supplied value). Unless the application explicitly calls initialSettings().maxConcurrentStreams(n), a Netty HTTP\/2 server advertises no limit and enforces none locally. Each open stream allocates a DefaultStream object, PropertyMap slots, flow-controller state and IntObjectHashMap entry; with ~2^30 permissible odd stream IDs a single TCP connection can create hundreds of thousands of long-lived stream objects. This is also the precondition for CVE-2023-44487-style Rapid-Reset amplification, where the absence of a low concurrent cap multiplies backend work. Versions 4.1.135.Final and 4.2.15.Final patch the issue."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "vulnerabilityHash": "5dc002d4eb8dfbf0cebb2b5b3a4e3763"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
